When the first Luminar emerged from the skies of Melvedor, none knew from whence it came, nor what purpose had stirred it from the unseen realms beyond. Yet a Luminar was unlike any creature that the world had ever beheld.
Its form was harder than the Alris steel wrought by the delving dwarves in their deep forges, its flames burned with a fury greater than the forges of men beneath the Galris volcano, its wisdom outshone the high councils of the elves in far Valdaris, and its might in battle was such that even the angels of the celestial host withdrew in awe to their high palaces beyond the stars. Such was the power and majesty of a Luminar.
Overwhelmed by this dread and splendour, and already weary from centuries of strife, Melvedor was ill-prepared for so fell a visitation. Its peoples looked upon the coming of the Luminar as the herald of their utter ending. In their desperation the seven kingdoms forged the first and only truce among them, uniting all their hosts in a last hopeless stand to cast down the mighty being.
But they were melted as wax before a furnace. From that day forth the world belonged to Elerus, the first Luminar.
As tidings of the utter defeat spread through the realms, hope perished and chaos reigned, for no power remained that could guard the lands from ruin. Yet Elerus, feared above all things, sought no further foes. He ceased his ravaging of field and mountain, and lo! the wars of the kingdoms themselves fell silent. Thus began a century of unlooked-for peace.
Before he departed into the veiled heights, Elerus bestowed upon the realm seven Luminar eggs, one for each kingdom. With this gift came the first revelation of the Luminari's secret: that these great spirits must be bound in soul to a living being—a newborn child—and reared together as one. This sacred union is called the Luminis. To each kingdom was granted its own Luminis, that the chosen child and the egg might grow as kin beneath the same roof.
Much concerning the nature and workings of the Luminis bond remains veiled in mystery. Yet it is known that, when the bonded reach the fullness of their years, they may journey together in sleep to Nodheart, the hidden realm of dreams. There, in the halls of vision, the Council of the Seven shall one day convene, and the seven shall take counsel with one another upon the peace of the kingdoms.
With peace came prosperity, and with prosperity came power, and with power came the ancient temptation to wield it unwisely. And so, in the absence of Elerus and while Nodheart yet lay beyond mortal reach, a new and shadowed age began to stir.
When dark whispers spread through the lands that the necromancers of the undead realm were employing forbidden arts to hasten the hatching of their Luminar, the hosts of the Boldstone King marched upon their necropolis in wrath.
The battle was swift and seemed a victory for the men of Boldstone. Yet it was no true triumph. For the victors, seeking to strengthen their own ranks by the same black sorcery, lingered long within the citadel and delved into its accursed secrets. The longer they remained, the more their souls were corrupted, until at last they too became numbered among the undead.
Burning with vengeance for the ruin that had befallen him, the corrupted general Nendalras of the once-human army raised a crusade against the kingdom of Boldstone. With his forces greatly diminished, the Boldstone King entrusted his youngest daughter, Cestia, with her own Luminar egg and bade her flee into the wilds, seeking refuge in another realm.
Alas, the far-sighted Nendalras had already sent forth a swift scout-host to waylay her. Ere she could discern any path of escape, the shadow of his riders fell across her road, and the princess found herself hunted beneath the darkening sky.